<interrogationes unitas="chorda">

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass chorda.datum to printf, strcmp or fopen?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_ut_cstr chorda_aequalis_literis">No:
      a chorda is mensura plus datum with NO terminating NUL, so C
      string functions read past the end. chorda_ut_cstr copies the
      bytes plus a NUL into the piscina; for printing without a copy
      use printf("%.*s", (int)s.mensura, s.datum). chorda_ut_cstr
      returns NIHIL (not "") when datum is NIHIL, which is how many
      functions here represent an empty result, so check before
      handing it to printf.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make a chorda from a C string literal, and does it copy?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_ex_literis chorda_ex_buffer chorda_aequalis_literis">chorda_ex_literis
      copies the bytes into the piscina (an empty literal gives
      {0, NIHIL}, because piscina_allocare of zero bytes returns
      NIHIL). chorda_ex_buffer wraps an existing i8 buffer without
      copying, but takes a non-const pointer, so a literal needs a
      cast that -Wcast-qual refuses; several files keep a private
      _chorda_ex_cstr view helper for that. To merely compare against a
      literal, chorda_aequalis_literis needs no chorda at all.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what counts as an empty chorda, and why do {0, NIHIL} and {0, pointer} behave differently?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_vacua chorda_continet chorda_incipit chorda_invenire chorda_ut_cstr">Both
      have mensura 0, and chorda_vacua is VERUM for either, so test
      emptiness with it rather than with datum == NIHIL. The search
      functions do not treat them alike: with a NIHIL-datum needle
      chorda_continet and chorda_incipit say FALSUM and chorda_invenire
      finds nothing, while a zero-length needle with a real pointer
      matches at once (chorda_invenire then returns the whole
      haystack). chorda_ut_cstr gives NIHIL for the first and "" for
      the second.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep a slice or a trimmed chorda after the original buffer is gone?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_sectio chorda_praecidere chorda_praecidi_laterale chorda_fissio chorda_transcribere">No:
      chorda_sectio, chorda_praecidere (and _sinistram, _dextram),
      chorda_invenire and the elements from chorda_fissio all point
      into the original bytes. chorda_praecidi_laterale also returns a
      view even though it takes a piscina (and returns empty if that
      piscina is NIHIL). Copy with chorda_transcribere into a piscina
      that lives long enough.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find a substring and get the text after it?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_invenire chorda_invenire_index chorda_sectio chorda_continet chorda_invenire_ultimum_index">chorda_invenire
      returns only the MATCHED slice (mensura = needle length), not the
      rest of the string the way strstr does. For the rest, take
      chorda_invenire_index (an s32, -1 when absent) and cut with
      chorda_sectio(s, index + acus.mensura, s.mensura). For a plain
      yes/no use chorda_continet; the _ultimum variants search from the
      end.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I split a string on a delimiter and join the pieces back?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_fissio chorda_fissio_chorda chorda_iungere">chorda_fissio
      splits on one character, chorda_fissio_chorda on a multi-byte
      delimiter; both return a chorda_fissio_fructus (elementa,
      numerus) whose elements are views into the input. Empty fields
      are kept: "a,,b," gives four elements, the last one empty. An
      empty input gives zero elements, and an empty delimiter to
      chorda_fissio_chorda gives the whole input as one element.
      chorda_iungere joins an array with a separator into a new
      chorda. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): chorda_fissio_chorda
      reads past the end of the input and crashes whenever the
      delimiter is longer than the input ("a" split on "abc"), and
      chorda_fissio never matches a delimiter byte of 0x80 or above
      (0xFF, 0xC3) because character is signed on arm64 macOS, so such
      an input comes back as one element.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse an integer or a float out of a chorda safely?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_ut_s32 chorda_ut_i32 chorda_ut_s64 chorda_ut_f64">Use
      chorda_ut_s32, chorda_ut_i32, chorda_ut_s64 or chorda_ut_f64:
      each returns b32 and writes the result only on success. The whole
      chorda must be the number, so "12x", an embedded NUL byte, an
      empty chorda and out-of-range values are refused. Small
      differences: _s32 and _i32 go through strtol/strtoul and accept
      leading whitespace, _s64 is hand-written and accepts only an
      optional sign and digits, and _i32 refuses a leading '-'. For a
      number at the front of a longer text, cut it out with
      chorda_sectio first. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): _i32
      checks for '-' only at the first byte, so after leading
      whitespace a minus is accepted and wrapped modulo 2^64: " -0"
      gives VERUM 0 and "\t-18446744069414584321" gives VERUM
      4294967295, while "-0" is refused.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a number into a chorda?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_ex_s32 chorda_ex_s64 chorda_ex_f64 chorda_ex_f64_exacta chorda_aedificator_appendere_s32">chorda_ex_s32
      and chorda_ex_s64 write decimal integers. chorda_ex_f64(n,
      praecisio, piscina) writes fixed decimals and returns an EMPTY
      chorda when praecisio is above L or the text would not fit its
      CXXXII-byte buffer (1e300 at six places does not), so large
      values vanish silently. chorda_ex_f64_exacta writes %.17g, which
      reads back to the same double. Inside a larger text, append with
      the chorda_aedificator functions instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compare chordae for equality or for sorting?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aequalis chorda_aequalis_literis chorda_aequalis_case_insensitivus chorda_comparare">chorda_aequalis
      compares length then bytes; chorda_aequalis_literis compares with
      a C string without allocating; chorda_aequalis_case_insensitivus
      folds through tolower, byte by byte, so only ASCII letters fold.
      For ordering, chorda_comparare is memcmp over the common prefix,
      then the shorter string first; it returns memcmp's own value
      when the bytes differ, so test its sign rather than comparing
      with -1 or 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I convert an identifier between snake_case, camelCase, PascalCase and kebab-case?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_serpens chorda_camelus chorda_pascalis chorda_kebab chorda_pascalis_serpens">Use
      chorda_serpens, chorda_camelus, chorda_pascalis, chorda_kebab or
      chorda_pascalis_serpens. All split the input into words the same
      way: any non-alphanumeric byte separates, a lower-to-upper step
      starts a word, a letter after a digit starts a word, and a run of
      capitals followed by a lowercase letter gives its last capital to
      the next word, so "XMLParser"
      becomes xml_parser and "abc123def" becomes abc123_def. Case is
      changed with toupper and tolower, so this is ASCII only.
      Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): non-ASCII letters are not
      kept but dropped as separators, so UTF-8 "café au lait" becomes
      caf_au_lait, CafAuLait and caf-au-lait (chorda_kebab slugs for
      "Café" and "Caf" collide).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use chorda_ex_bytes_legibilis for binary (KiB, MiB) sizes?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_ex_bytes_legibilis">No:
      it divides by M, which is 1000, so the units are decimal: 1000
      bytes gives "1.0 KB" and 1023 bytes is also "1.0 KB", not "1023
      B". The header's examples 1024 and 1048576 only look binary
      because they round to the same text, and its 1073741824 example
      is wrong: that value prints "1.1 GB". Values under one hundred
      get one decimal place, larger values none.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I hash a chorda, and is the hash stable across runs?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_friare tabula_dispersa_creare_chorda">chorda_friare
      is 32-bit FNV-1a over the bytes (i32, unsigned) with no seed, so
      the same bytes give the same hash in every run and on every
      machine. For a string-keyed map use tabula_dispersa rather than
      hashing by hand.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I replace every occurrence of a substring?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_substituere chorda_numerare_occurrentia">chorda_substituere(s,
      antiquum, novum, piscina) scans left to right and replaces
      non-overlapping matches into a new chorda (chorda_numerare_occurrentia
      counts the same way: "aaaa" holds two "aa"). An empty antiquum
      returns a plain copy; a result that comes out empty is
      {0, NIHIL}. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): the result
      length is never checked against i32, so a growing replacement
      whose result exceeds 4 GiB (65536 'a' with antiquum "a" and a
      65537-byte novum) wraps, allocates too little and writes past
      the buffer.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
